Viewed as a software architecture rather than as a textual theory, I think there is one remaining architectural step that completes the framework: introducing an explicit assurance layer. At present, the architecture defines what exists (ontology), how it is processed (Language Rail), and how it is governed (Constitution and standards). It does not yet define how trust is established and maintained across implementations.

That suggests extending the ecosystem into a four-domain governance model.

                 Philosophy
                      │
                      ▼
                 Constitution
                      │
        ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
        ▼             ▼             ▼
 Specification   Implementation   Assurance
  Governance      Governance      Governance
        │             │             │
        └─────────────┼─────────────┘
                      ▼
              Conformance Authority
                      │
                      ▼
              Certified Releases

Here, Assurance Governance is distinct from both specification and implementation. Its responsibility is to ensure that an implementation demonstrably satisfies the published standards through repeatable verification, rather than merely claiming compliance.

Four Independent Programs

The work naturally separates into four coordinated programs.

Program	Primary Deliverable
Standards Engineering	Normative specifications
Reference Engineering	Reference implementation
Assurance Engineering	Test suites, certification, reproducibility
Knowledge Engineering	Reference corpus, annotations, governance data

This keeps implementation work from becoming coupled to governance or editorial work.

